      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/gists{/gist_id}",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/starred{/owner}{/repo}",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/flackr/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "body": "Yes, I had imagined that this should run once after at least the first round of resize observers so that animations could use the size set by the resizeobserver in the initially rendered frame.\r\n\r\n> resize observers will not see scroll-animations, scroll-state queries, and anchor-positioning taken into account\r\n\r\nThis is really tricky, however I think for the use cases we have now, running after makes more sense.\r\n* scroll driven animations usually care more about observing the output of resize observers and are not likely to change resize observer determining things since that is inefficient to animate.\r\n* scroll-state queries again are usually used for subtle non size affecting things and are more likely to depend on the resize observer output as that may determine whether something is e.g. stuck.\r\n* anchor positioning is the one case where i could see it often being used to determine a size, however given that we already allow for a stale size on the compositor it seems like it wouldn't be too unexpected to have it not update until the next frame.",
    "created_at": "2025-09-08T13:27:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-08T13:27:34Z",
